\subsection{Finding a Shortest Word That Distinguishes Two DFAs}

An implementation can compare two automata without minimizing either
one. Build the reachable part of their product graph on demand
and look for a pair with unequal acceptance bits. The inputs are
complete deterministic automata over the same finite alphabet.
At a product state $(p,q)$, define $\mathrm{step}$ on a symbol
to apply each automaton's transition to its own component.
Store a parent pair and an incoming symbol when a pair is first
discovered. The answer is language equality or a shortest word
accepted by exactly one automaton; among equally short words,
the scan order of the alphabet determines the tie-break.

\begin{sudocode}[Breadth-first search of the product graph]
\Function{Distinguish}{$s,\Sigma$}
  \State Enqueue start pair $s$; set $P[s]\gets\mathrm{root}$
  \While{the queue is not empty}
    \State $u\gets$ dequeue next pair
    \If{$u$ has unequal acceptance bits}
      \State \Return word reconstructed using $P$
      \Comment{Read edge labels from root to $u$}
    \EndIf
    \ForAll{$a\in\Sigma$ in scan order}
      \State $v\gets\mathrm{step}(u,a)$
      \If{$v\notin\operatorname{dom}(P)$}
        \State $P[v]\gets(u,a)$; enqueue $v$
        \Comment{Keep first, shortest parent path}
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndWhile
  \State \Return equal
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
Over $\{a\}$, compare an automaton for even length with one
for length divisible by three. Their states count letters modulo
two and three respectively, and each accepts only at state zero.
Start at $(0,0)$, where both accept. On \texttt{a}, discover
$(1,1)$, where both reject. Its outgoing edge discovers
$(0,2)$, which accepts on the first side only. Following
the two parent edges reconstructs \texttt{aa}. Neither the empty
word nor \texttt{a} distinguishes the automata, as their visited
pairs show.

\Cref{fig:visual-33} shows where the product search first finds unequal
acceptance bits.

