\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
Each visited pair represents the exact two states reached by its
parent word, and enumeration in breadth-first order ensures that
no shorter distinguishing word was overlooked. Exhausting all
reachable pairs with equal flags proves language equality;
this is not testing a sample of words. The worst case visits
$|Q_1||Q_2|$ pairs and checks one transition per symbol per pair.
If the start flags differ, the correct witness is the empty word.

\subsection{The Pumping Lemma}
\label{sec:pumping-lemma}

The pumping lemma provides a necessary condition for a language to be regular. It is used to prove that certain languages are \emph{not} regular.

\begin{theorem}[Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages]
\label{thm:pumping-lemma}
If $L$ is a regular language, then there exists a constant $p \geq 1$ (the \textbf{pumping length}) such that every string $s \in L$ with $|s| \geq p$ can be written as $s = xyz$ where:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $|y| \geq 1$
  \item $|xy| \leq p$
  \item For all $i \geq 0$, $xy^iz \in L$
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $M$ be a DFA recognizing $L$ with $p = |Q|$ states. If $|s| \geq p$, then reading $s$ visits at least $p + 1$ states, so some state must be repeated by the pigeonhole principle. Let the repeated state be visited after reading $x$ and after reading $xy$. Then $y$ can be pumped.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Non-Regular Language]
\label{ex:non-regular}
The language $L = \{a^n b^n \mid n \geq 0\}$ is not regular.

\textit{Proof}: Suppose $L$ is regular with pumping length $p$. Consider $s = a^p b^p \in L$. By the pumping lemma, $s = xyz$ with $|xy| \leq p$ and $|y| \geq 1$. Since $|xy| \leq p$, $y$ consists entirely of $a$'s. Pumping $y$ gives $xy^2z = a^{p+|y|} b^p$, which has more $a$'s than $b$'s and is not in $L$. Contradiction.
\end{example}

\subsection{The Myhill--Nerode Theorem}
\label{sec:myhill-nerode}

\begin{definition}[Myhill--Nerode Relation]
\label{def:myhill-nerode}
The \textbf{Myhill--Nerode relation} $\equiv_L$ for a language $L$ is defined by:
\[
x \equiv_L y \iff \forall z \in \Sigma^*.\; (xz \in L \iff yz \in L)
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Myhill--Nerode Theorem]
\label{thm:myhill-nerode}
A language $L$ is regular if and only if $\equiv_L$ has finitely many equivalence classes. Moreover, the number of equivalence classes equals the number of states in the minimal DFA for $L$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Transducers: Mealy and Moore Machines}
\label{sec:transducers}

\subsection{Mealy Machines}
\label{sec:mealy}

\begin{definition}[Mealy Machine]
\label{def:mealy}
A \textbf{Mealy machine} is a 6-tuple $(Q, \Sigma, \Delta, \delta, \lambda, q_0)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $Q$, $\Sigma$, $\delta$, $q_0$ are as for DFAs
  \item $\Delta$ is the \textbf{output alphabet}
  \item $\lambda : Q \times \Sigma \to \Delta$ is the \textbf{output function}
\end{itemize}
The output depends on both the current state and the current input.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Moore Machines}
\label{sec:moore}

\begin{definition}[Moore Machine]
\label{def:moore}
A \textbf{Moore machine} is a 6-tuple $(Q, \Sigma, \Delta, \delta, \lambda, q_0)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $Q$, $\Sigma$, $\delta$, $q_0$ are as for DFAs
  \item $\Delta$ is the \textbf{output alphabet}
  \item $\lambda : Q \to \Delta$ is the \textbf{output function}
\end{itemize}
The output depends only on the current state.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Mealy--Moore Equivalence]
\label{thm:mealy-moore}
For every Mealy machine, there exists an equivalent Moore machine (producing the same output sequence, shifted by one position), and vice versa.
\end{theorem}

\section{Implementation Considerations}
\label{sec:fa-implementation}

\subsection{Table-Driven Implementation}
\label{sec:table-driven}

A DFA can be implemented as a two-dimensional table $\texttt{table}[Q][\Sigma]$:

\begin{verbatim}
  state = q0
  for each character c in input:
      state = table[state][c]
  if state in F:
      accept
  else:
      reject
\end{verbatim}

This approach is simple and efficient for small alphabets but requires $|Q| \times |\Sigma|$ space.

\subsection{Compact Representations}
\label{sec:compact-repr}

For large DFAs, several compact representations are used:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Default transitions}: Most transitions go to a ``default'' state; only exceptions are stored.
  \item \textbf{Compressed tables}: Transitions are encoded as ranges or bitmaps.
  \item \textbf{Sparse representations}: Only non-default transitions are stored, using hash maps or sorted arrays.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:fa-summary}

Finite automata and regular languages form the foundation of lexical analysis and pattern matching. The key results include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The equivalence of DFAs, NFAs, and regular expressions (Kleene's theorem).
  \item The subset construction for converting NFAs to DFAs.
  \item DFA minimization algorithms (Hopcroft's algorithm).
  \item The pumping lemma and Myhill--Nerode theorem for characterizing regular languages.
  \item Transducers (Mealy and Moore machines) for modeling finite-state computations with output.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The theory of finite automata is one of the oldest areas of theoretical computer science. From its origins in the work of Kleene, Rabin, and Scott in the 1950s, it has grown to encompass a vast body of results and applications. Modern developments include: weighted automata for probabilistic and quantitative verification; symbolic automata for handling large alphabets (e.g., Unicode); streaming automata for processing infinite inputs; and the application of automata theory to model checking and formal verification.
\end{historical}

